A well-hidden Cave by a hermit in the woods near Nový Bor wasn't really habitable
In the north of Bohemia in the foothills of the Lusatian Mountains, near Nový Bor in the Česká Lípa region, there are several sandblasphemous rock formations hidden in the forests led by the Hiking Road.
Natural and man-generated.
And we're going to follow that human hand created.
The place is called Cavity at the Hermit.
In fact, there has never been a hermit and the cave served as a hiding place, more precisely a hiding chamber apparently for grain.
The Cave is carved from the side into a rock promontory and accessible by stairs from above.
At the same time, until the very last moment, it is practically not visible.
Revealed by a narrow stair stairs leading to a kind of rock gallery, from where it is just getting into the rock.
Although it seems that the ventricle is quite far away from human dwellings, at the time of its incorporation it may have been on a plateau, where there is a forest today.
"The fact that the building does not have a window, and even in a traditional place located firebox clearly proves that no hermit could ever survive the winter here," explains historian Kamil Podroužek of the Philosophical Faculty of Jan Evangelista Purkyně in Ústí nad Labem.
The one-space object at the back has only a stone peg, apparently used to remove the containers.
"I can see it as a chamber for storing some food or products from the field," adds Kamil Podroužek.
On the walls of the cave there are visible traces of tools.
Breaking off the sandstone canines was quite simple and fast for insiders.
It was enough to properly use the joints and the sandstone gradually break off.
Depending on the length of the canine trace and the method of chopping in general, it is possible to determine the period of the formation of the cave.
According to the historian, this is probably the end of the 18-th century.
The cave is freely accessible, and according to the mess that appears in it, someone sometimes sleeps there in these days.
