Environmental Activists Were Prosecuted in Prague
According to the organizers, the government's current actions on the protection and recovery of the Czech landscape and the solution of the climate crisis are underdecreasing; the speakers have called on the state for a more vigorous approach.
Representatives of large organizations such as the Rainbow Movement, Greenpeace and the Green Party, as well as smaller local associations, took part in an event titled The Last Drop.
"We have here, for example, people who have been performing for a long time against the expansion at the Turów mine, there are people who are speaking out against the mining of the gravedigger near Uherské Ostroh, there are people who oppose the construction of nonsensical dams," said Marika Volfová on behalf of the organizers.
It stated that protesters' main demands are the creation of a plan for water retention in the landscape, a vigorous change in agricultural policy and a subsidy system towards promoting sustainable agriculture and the transition from fossil fuels to renewable energy as soon as possible.
"The government is supposed to defend the interests of the people, not reckless and devastating business," she said.
There was a booth with a petition inviting representatives of the Czech state to work on reducing greenhouse gas emissions and the restoration of Czech land and landscape.
The people present were equipped with banners with slogans such as "End of rapeseed depressants" or "Minus coal, more newts".
From South Moravia, Jan Skoda came to Prague from the association For Water for People, which is protesting the plan of gravel mining in the Hodonín region.
"It jeopardizes a source of drinking water for 140 000 people," he said.
He added that boreholes represent the fifth-largest source of drinking water in the Czech Republic.
According to him, the planned gravel mining is located only about 500 meters from boreholes, and according to the testimonies, it is certain that the quality of the water will be endangered.
Vojtěch Veselý came for the so-called agro-ecological farmer from the village of Valeč in the Karlovy Vary region.
He drew attention to the prowess of small farms that do not marrge on revenues and profits and supply residents on the spot.
"Small agro-ecological farms are able to feed the entire population completely normally, on top of that, they do not cause such destruction on the form of the countryside and the environment," he said.
Thousands killed in plane crash on house in Germany
Three people were killed in an ultra-threscumble on an apartment building in western Germany today and two others were injured.
This was reported by the local emergency services, according to which among the dead are probably two crew members and one resident of the house.
The aircraft crashed onto the roof of a building in Wesel, North Rhine-Vestphalia.
The attic apartment, which was one of the five residential units in the house, subsequently engulfed a blaze.
Firefighters have already managed to bring the blaze under control.
Local fire brigade commander Robert Meyboom reported that both crew members of the aircraft may have died.
According to the police, the third victim is a woman who resided in the house.
Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung (WAZ), citing Mayor Wesel, wrote that she was a single-moving mother whose two-year-old daughter was bailed from the apartment with slight injuries.
An emergency parachute has been found near the accident, which probably served as a brake on landing.
The police assume that they came from the crashed plane.
There was a place in the aerodrome for two people.
The machine lifted off from an airport in the nearby town of Marl.
Rheinische Post, citing eclipsed witnesses, said the plane collided with a hot-air balloon before the crash.
However, this information has not yet been endorsed.
WAZ, citing other testimonies, wrote that the aircraft disintegrated into the air before hitting the ground.
Česká spořitelna erroneously charged some card payments twice on Saturday.
