Allegations Argued in Nantes Due to Cathedral Fiat, Man Confesses
The Cathedral of St. Peter and Paul in the heart of Nantes began to burn last Saturday and the fire seriously damaged the original 16th-century stained stained glass windows and destroyed an organ.
Suspicions of arsition were veiled by investigators because the blaze advancing through the temple from three different locations.
At the same time, the police did not find any signs at the entrances that someone would get into the cathedral by force.
Accusation is a volunteer with a local diocese, a thirty-nine-year-old Rwandan fugitive.
The police arrested him shortly after the blaze, but then dismissed him without prosecuting him.
Prosecutors have now reported that on the basis of new evidence, the man was re-assured on Saturday, pleaded guilty and faces arguing allegations.
He faces up to ten years imprisonment and a hefty fine.
According to his lawyer, the man regrets the crime and decided to cooperate fully with the investigators, Le Monde wrote.
Vojtěch: On Monday we will introduce a "semaphore" for epidemiological measures
The new system is intended to fulfil the role of a pandemic plan, due to the lack of which the government and the Ministry of Health face criticism.
"Grades will be according to the strength of the community spread," the minister explained.
He added that, for example, the Moravian-Silesian Region or Prague would currently reach a central, orange stage.
The system is designed as a regional, because the situation in individual counties varies significantly, added.
The Ministry cooperated with epidemiologists and the Institute of Health Instencies (ÚZIS).
According to the Minister, specific measures will be linked to individual stages, whether it is an obligation to wear masks or the organization of special departments in hospitals.
Minister of the Interior Jan Hamáček (ČSSD) urged to create an epidemiological plan on the Prima Partie television program, according to which people should have clear information about what measures they can expect if an epidemiological situation deteriorated.
The daily increase in confirmed cases in the Czech Republic has risen in recent days, although the number of hospitalized remains low.
Vojtěch commented on the fact that there is nothing fundamental happening to speak of declaring a state of emergency or relaunch of the crisis staff.
With regard to the growing number of people infected since Saturday throughout the Czech Republic, the government has once again introduced an obligation to wear drapes at indoor events of more than 100 people.
As of Monday the capacity of these events will be limited to 500 people, now it amounts to 1,000 people.
The procedure of the government, when until the last moment it was not clear what measures and when they would come into force even when they would be announced, was criticized.
"I don't think that in a situation that's quite tense, on the contrary, the main actors should create a sense of security and straighten the confusion," Senator Marek Hilšer said.
Precautions may already vary according to the region, for example in Prague from Sunday there is an obligation to wear masks even in non-residential health care facilities, such as pharmaceuticals or waiting rooms at a doctor.
The obligation to wear drapes while visiting medical and social and pharmacies has also been introduced since Friday in the Liberec and Usti regions.
It is necessary to wear drapes in indoor public areas in the Vysočina in the Jihlova region, in public transport and in medical care facilities from 14 July.
Conversely, anti-epidemic measures in the Moravian-Silesian Region were relaxed, as in the Central Bohemian Region in the Kutná Hora region.
Already at the beginning of June, the Czech government also approved the so-called semaphore for Europe, which colorfully differentiates the degree of risk of contagion in individual European countries and accordingly regulates the rules valid for entry into the Czech Republic from these countries.
For low- and medium-hazardous countries, from June 15, the authorities do not require a negative coronavirus test or do not order quarantine from Czech citizens on June 15.
These are obligatory in the return of Czechs from high-risk countries.
At this time, all EU Member States are now in the low-concern category.
Technoparty in the Benešov region continues.
He's playing one-in-a-three-dollar capacity, she was supposed to end at noon
Hlaváčková Lhota (Benešovsko) Technoparty near Hlaváčková Lhota in the Benešov region continues on Sunday evening.
One-third of the original 12 musical systems are still playing.
According to evening estimations, about 500 people remain here after 20:00, the original rough estimations talked about three hundred, in the morning there were 900, police spokeswoman Lucie Nováková said.
It was originally supposed to end at noisy at noon on Sunday, at that time some of the musical systems actually fell silent.
"Some have already left and others are about to leave.
The musical production in part still persists.
We are expected to gradually detach and disassemble music systems," Nováková said.
"Policol officers are still consistently inspecting departing visitors in order to prevent drivers from driving under the influence of alcohol or addictive substances.
Drivers have the opportunity to undergo a preemptive breathing test before leaving," she added.
The event is supervised by dozens of riot police officers, on the scene there are foreign police, traffic and riot police, and dog handlers with service dogs are also on stand-alone.
"During the present day, police officers inspected 470 people moving at the venue and 485 vehicles. 270 drivers were subjected to a breath test, six of whom sat behind the wheel under the influence of liquor," Nováková said.
A total of 95 drivers were tested by police for the presence of intoxicated and psychotropic substances, of which one was positive.
The police also dealt with 14 offenses in transport.
At the request of technoparty participants, officers carried out 300 preventive breath tests and four preventive tests for the presence of intoxicating and psychotropic substances.
There were about 2000 people at the event on Saturday morning.
If the number of attendees didn't subdue, the police would have intervened there.
