Putin on the phone with Zelensky before the ceasefire in Donbas, the Kremlin criticises planned elections
U.S. President Vladimir Putin spoke by phone with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky about the situation in Donbas, where a new ceasefire is scheduled to take effect from midnight.
According to the Kremlin's statement, among other things he criticized the Ukrainian parliament's decision to hold local elections; Kiev reported that Zelensky has called for detained Ukrainians to be released.
Negotiators of Ukraine and Russia with the support of the Organization for Security and Co-Cooperation in Europe (OSCE) agreed on a new peace of arms on Wednesday evening.
Ceasefire is due to begin after midnight on Monday night, it should be "complete and general" and to open the way to comply with further provisions of the peace agreements.
Speaking to Zelensky, Putin told Zelensky that Ukraine's parliament's decision to hold local elections is inconsistent with a peace treaty from Minsk and jeopardizing the prospects of separating the conflict in the east of Ukraine.
The Ukrainian parliament ruled last week that the forthfinity elections for local councils would not take place in territory dominated by pro-Russian insurgencies.
The Minsk agreement envisages that Kiev will reincarnate under the control of the borders of the Ukrainian landoccupied separatists with Russia after it provides broad autonomy to these regions and hold local elections.
According to Unian news agency, Zelensky's office said the two statesmen agreed on the benefits of the forthcoming ceasefire in Donbas and the need to take further measures to promote peace in the region, such as de-mining areas of the fighting or withdrawing part of troops from the queue.
Zelensky also urged Putin to be releasing Ukrainians held in the east of Ukraine, in the Occupied Peninsula of Crimea even in Russia.
The Minsk treaty helped stop the fighting, but in occasional firefights and artillery attacks on both sides of the queue, people continue to die.
Conflict in the Donbas region has killed more than 14,000 people since the spring of 2014.
Ukraine and the West accuse Russia of mis-administering separatists, which Moscow denies.
Zelensky has made an end-to-end war in the Donbas, but there has not yet been a more fundamental breakthrough in the negotiations.
