organic LPG arrived in the Czech Republic, promises ecological operation
liquefied Petroleum Gas, abbreviation LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas), is a very well-known alternative fuel in the Czech Republic.
It is especially popular because of its fundamentally lower price compared to gasoline or diesel, a significantly lower exciuation tax, but its benefit is even in lower CO2 emissions per kilometer.
Similar to gasoline and diesel, it also has an eco-friendly option - "bio LPG".
However, it is quite a novelty - it first appeared on the European market last year and, according to a press release from the Czech LPG association, arrived in the Czech Republic a few days ago.
"In these days we received the first fifteen tons of organic LPG, which will be distributed to our petrol stations in a mixture with a classic fossil LPG," Jiří Karlík of Primagas is quoted as quoting.
Organic LPG, however, can not be considered as bioethanol or biodiesel.
According to the association, it is chemically identical to the classic oil LPG, which a number of motorists are refuelling into their cars now.
That’s why it is also possible to mix both versions freely.
"The only difference compared to classic LPG is the method of production," Ivan Indráček, the chairman of the association, said about organic LPG.
This is one of the great advantages of organic LPG compared to other alternative fuels.
It is easy to be transported and stored, as well as the distribution infrastructure is quite good, e.g. compared to hydrogen or electricity.
This gas is currently obtained as a by-product in the production of bioethethanol from food and sugar canoe remnants.
In the future it should also be produced in a targeted way, e.g. from cellulose biomass, i.e. from wooden waste.
On the other hand, conventional oil LPG is produced partly as a by-product of oil refining, partly by separation during the extraction of natural gas.
As a result, depending on the production technology of production and the source, the operation of the car to organic LPG is 40-70% more environmentally thwarting in terms of the carbon footprint than operating on LPG oil and up to 90% compared to diesel.
However, it is not CO2 emissions from the exhaust of the car, but about the ecology of fuel production, the so-called "well-to-tank" (English "from the source to the tank").
One of the world's largest producers of bio LPG currently include petrochemical companies Eni, Total and Neste.
According to a recent update of the National Clean Mobility Action Plan (NAP ČM), Unipetrol is also exploring the possibility of production at its Litvínov refinery.
According to estimates of the association in the Czech Republic, about 170 thousand vehicles run on LPG; the Ministry of the Environment lists a slightly higher number in NAP ČM, about 200 thousand.
According to the statistics of the Ministry of Transport alone, almost 6,000 conversions of LPG cars were carried out last year alone.
It is possible to refuel LPG in the Czech Republic at about 950 stations of different operators.
Vojtěch revised the number of infected, the number fell by 1775 cases
Health Minister Adam Vojtěch (for ANO) ordered the ministry to revise the numbers infected with the coronavirus virus.
