Protests in Buharsko: Protesters threw pig's ears at the government's building
Participants of the rally against the Bulgarian Prime Minister Bojek Borisov and his Cabinet threw swine ears at the government building.
It was another in a series of protests against an "mafia-style governance," which has been taking place in Sophia and other cities for almost three weeks.
Organizers are planning a nation-wide blockade on Wednesday, DPA news agency said.
Today's protest, which took place on the 18-th day in a row, had a weaker turnout according to Radio Free Europe/Rádio Svoboda.
As of 20:00 about 300 people gathered, some streets had to be enclosed for traffic.
Demonstrators in front of the government's building played a theatrical skeleton, in which they symbolically locked Prime Minister Borisov and Prosecutor General Ivan Geshev in connection with the Arreviation of two advisers of President Rumen Radev, who is a strong political opponent of the prime minister.
Responsibilities of both are among the basic demands of the protesters.
They also demanded the abolition of announcing the announced health care reform and the end of corruption under construction on the Black Sea coast.
Prostations organizer Nikolai Hajigenov said on public radio today that there is a "top escalation" on Wednesday in the form of "a nationwide demonstration in Sophia and blockades across the country".
He reiterated the demand for the resignation of Borissov and his government, as well as announcing an early-early election.
Procedest ones should take place in March 2021.
Borissov has previously said that he is not going to resign.
This was justified, among other things, by the need to transvene Bulgaria through the Covid-19 pandemic and the resulting economic crisis.
On Friday, however, he exchanged five members of his governing team, although he did not reassume the protesters.
Earlier this week, Borissov's government survived a vote of confidence in parliament in parliament.
Coach Boloni Mihajlovič no longer wants matches without spectators
Football coach Boloni Sinisha Mihajlovič already has had enough Italian league matches without spectators.
The Serbian coach hopes that fans will return to the stadiums since the new season.
"What we're doing right now isn't football.
I'm fed up with matches without spectators.
It's gender-free football, it doesn't bring me any emotion, no joy.
Those games are worse than training.
Playing like this is terrible and I don't think I'm the only one who thinks so," Mihajlovič said ahead of today's match against Lecce.
After the coronavirus hiatus there are Série A matches in quick success, and although they are played only in the evening, their actors are exposed to heat.
"We play every three days in a terrible heat.
You don’t even know how the teams are doing.
So that this season is over.
These measures have had to be taken in order to complete the competition, but I hope the fans will be back from September," said the coach, who in the past led Fiorentina and AC Milan.
For Mihajlović, this season is also extraordinary because it has been absent for four months on the bench due to leukemia treatment.
"Due to my sickness, breaking the league and everything else, we managed to move our team higher.
“We still want to continue to grow and for that we need the players to be mentally stronger,” he said.
We have become the target of cyber-attack of foreign states, according to Iran
From the end of June, a number of Iranian military, industrial and nuclear installations, as also oil refineries, power plants, factories and businesses have been reported fires and explosions.
Some Iranian officials have argued that a blaze that erupted this month in an under-terrestrial nuclear operation in Natanz may have feathered cyber sabotage.
A number of other incidents have not yet been explained.
Iranian Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Abbas Musawi told reporters, according to Iranian media, the infrastructure of the Islamic Republic faces "thousands of cyberattacks every day."
He added that it was not a new phenomenon and that most of it could be repulsed.
In recent months, according to Musawi, several wider cyberattacks have been recorded, behind which "certain governments and groups have been defended."
However, he did not give further details about possible perpetrators, according to Reuters.
Musáví also added that fires in forests, refineries and other places are frequent in the summer.
An article by Iran's IRNA news agency dealt this month about the fact that enemies of Iran, such as Israel and the U.S., may have stood behind recent incidents.
However, not one of these countries have been directly accused of specific attacks in the article.
Iran and the U.S. interrupted diplomatic ties shortly after the 1979 revolution when Iranian militant students attacked the U.S. embassies in Tehran and held hostage 52 Americans for 444 days.
Long-telected ties escalated between the two-degrees in 2018 after Washington withdrew from a 2015 nuclear deal between Iran and the world's powers and imposed sanctions against Iran that have a tough impact on its economy.
Another significant escalation came this year after the U.S. military on January 3 killed Iranian general Qassem Soleimani and an Iraqi militia member Abu Mahdi Muhandis.
On January 8, Iran fired a number of missiles at bases in Iraq, where the U.S. operates.
The U.S. only acknowledged that dozens of U.S. troops were wounded.
Israel sees Iran as its arch-enemy, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is the main advocacy of the 2015 international nuclear treaty with Iran.
According to the Israeli prime minister, Iran is trying to execute an atomic bomb, which Iran denies.
