Salaries in health care next year will rise by no more than five per cent.
According to Vojtěch, a bigger increase is unrealistic
Salaries in the health sector could increase by no more than five per cent next year, Health Minister Adam Vojtěch (for ANO) said in Václav Moravec on Czech Television.
He sees trade unionists' demand for an increase of up to 15 per cent unrealistic.
He also stated that the state should try to be self-sufficient in arranging protective equipment.
According to him, the hospitals now have their supplies.
"I don't think an increase (salaries) can be somewhere up to five per cent," the minister said.
According to him, it is necessary to reclamate the current situation, when it has intervened in the collection of the coronavirus pandemic insurance premiums and the government's regulations against its spread since March.
Revenues of insurers fell at the time.
"By a 15 per cent I get an unrealistic increase at this time," Vojtěch said.
The average monthly earnings of a doctor in acute inpatient care according to data from the Institute of Health Information and Statistics (ÚZIS) are likely to reach 88 312 kroner this year, the nurse 47 498 kroner.
The difference between the salary of nurses in state hospitals and the salary for other founders will be up to seven thousand crowns.
The ÚZIS data also shows that the loss of nurses in hospitals has stopped.
According to data from the Czech Statistical Office of the Czech Statistical Office, the average salaries in the Czech Republic was CZK 34-25.
Representatives of trade unionists already said in June that they will demand a minimum increase of ten per cent for 2021, ideally by 15 percent.
They criticize the long-term significant gap between the earnings of doctors and nurses in state-owned hospitals, which have to adhering to salary tables, and contractual salaries in hospitals of cities, counties or private.
Therefore, they also demand that the health caregivers be uniform for health care plates.
For nurses this year, the difference will reach up to seven thousand crowns per month.
For example, for doctors, wages grew faster than salaries last year.
According to Roman Šmucler, the president of the Czech Dentistry Committer, trade unionists should perceive how the state is at the moment.
Due to the effects of the Covid outbreak, the Chamber of graduation has recently approved an increase in this year's state budget deficit to a record CZK 500 billion, of which 1.75 billion is to go for the rewards of healthcare workers in hospitals and social services.
According to Šmucler, the council of health care providers does not agree with the widespread increase in salaries next year.
According to him, the remuneration should be decided by hospitals and medical establishments itself, and in addition, the money should go to them through a reimbursement decree.
Vojtěch also stated that the hospitals currently have enough protective equipment.
Pavel Švagr, Chairman of the Administration of State Material Reserves (SSHR), said this week that the administration currently does not have a single protective health aid.
It is awaiting the completion of tenders.
According to Vojtěch, suppliers in the world are now able to deliver goods better than in March, when they were redeemed.
According to him, the state should also try to be as self-sufficient as possible from Czech companies.
