Ostrava Mayor Macura won't run again, because he doesn't want to "fool in politics"
The current mayor of Ostrava Tomáš Macura (ANO) will not run in the next municipal elections.
The reason is an effort to "do not fool around in politics," he told news server Respekt.cz.
Macura, who has also been a member of the ANO movement's presidium, has been the mayor of Ostrava since November 2014.
In the municipal elections in 2018, the candidate of the ANO movement received almost a one-three electoral votes in Ostrava.
Macura originally only wanted to be in office for up to four years.
"I told myself that I would last a term of my tenure, and with the business experience, I'm going to be able to turn it all over and put everything on its feet during that time," Respekt said.
He added that for the second parliamentary term he was persuaded to a certain extent.
"But for me it's the maximum.
I don’t want to fool around in politics,” added Macura, who in an interview called himself a tough introvert who doesn’t have frequent contact with people.
According to Respekt for a long time, there has long been speculated about Macuro's possible ending in the ANO movement.
The server recalls that Macura has repeatedly criticized the ANO movement, for example for an alliance with the SPD movement or for supporting Miloš Zeman in the presidential elections.
As one of the few ANO personalities, he supported Zeman's anti-candidate Jiří Drahoš in the elections.
Macura doesn’t plan to retire from the ANO movement he joined in 2017.
"Since I have the feeling that I am able to change something in the movement, that it is not a completely futile struggle, and that no one dares, I do not have an imminent motive to quit," Respekt said.
Fifty-six-year-old Macura graduated from the University of Economics in Prague.
Earlier before he became the mayor of Ostrava in 2014, Macura worked, among other things, as the CEO of the pharmaceutical company Walmark.
He also sat in the bodies of other companies.
Retire to Brno by Uber offering transport services
At the end of July, the company Uber, which offers transport services, will return to Brno at the end of July.
This is the result of an amendment to the law, Petr Kratochvíl (ODS) said in a press statement on Thursday.
Taxi drivers objected against Uber in Brno, pointing out that Uber does not meet the conditions such as labels such as a taxi or a taximeter.
By changing the law, however, the conditions for the operation of regular taxis and alternative ones have changed since July.
Kratochvíl has held several talks in recent weeks with Uber representatives.
"On the basis of them, at the end of July this year the company decided to return to Brno with its services.
This step is the result of an amendment to the law, which makes it possible to legislature activities.
The city was acquainted with this change and we believe that the company will operate its services within given limits," Kratochvíl said.
Uber has worked in Brno in the past.
Taxi drivers protested against him because of non-labelling of vehicles as a taxi or a missing taximeter.
The court has issued a preliminary injunction that has banned Uber from driving in the city.
At the end of the year, Mayor Markéta Vaňková (ODS) said that the city would like to take steps for Uber to work in Brno.
According to subsequent decisions of the courts, Uber was able to drive in Brno, but the company did not take advantage of it.
It will now come back on the basis of an amendment to the law.
From July it is true that, in addition to classic taxis with a flashlight and a taximeter, it is easier to operate a transport service ordered and paid exclusively via mobile applications.
