Thousand Thousands Prosecute the Prime Minister
Thousands of Israelis have protested against unemployment, corruption and the way Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu addresses the coronavirus crisis.
Manifestations took place, among other things, before Netanyahu's private villa in Caesarea, near its official residency and in Tel Aviv.
Prostations have taken place regularly in Jerusalem and Tel Aviv for weeks.
Their attendees are demanding Netanyahu's ressignation because of a process indicted of corruption and fraud, as well as how the government is tackling another wave of the coronavirus outbreak.
"Bibi, go home," one of the demonstrators wrote on his banner, referring to Netanyahu's well-known nickname.
"Everyone can see that the emperor has lost his clothes," said another.
Seventy-year-old Netanyahu is the first prime minister of Israel to act.
Prosecuted in three cases of corruption, scudal and abuse of trust.
He denounces all accusations and calls them a witch hunt.
According to local media, the trial can take up to three years, more than 300 Witnesses are summoned.
SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus has been confirmed among nearly 60,500 people in Israel.
4,455 people have died in connection with the outbreak.
The Israeli government introduced quarantine measures in March to help reduce the daily increases of those infected.
After a gradual easing of restrictions from the end of April, especially at the end of June, the numbers of infected began to rise significantly again.
Netanyahu's critics say the government hasn't adequately helped self-sharvest-affiliates affected by quarantine measures.
Protesters are also annoyed by the fact that as part of the fight against the coronavirus, the government is issuing conflict-of-comparable instructions in the context of the fight against the coronavirus.
The driver smashed the car against a tree and perished.
The body had to be avoided from the wreck by firefighters
Tragic accident took place around 23 o'clock near Neveklov on a narrow district leading from the Slapy dam.
The driver apparently flew at considerable speed down into the forest and smashed the car against a tree.
He died on the spot.
The body had to be extricated by firefighters from the wreckage, among other things cutting off the roof.
Circumstances of the accident are being investigated by police.
They ordered a forensic autopsy, which should, among other things, clarify whether the man was running under the influence of alcohol or other addictive substances.
An end with wasting and chuking one collection after another.
Pietro Filipi abolishes discounts and responsives on sustainability
Czech clothing brand Pietro Filipi wants to focus on susceptibility and is embarking on a fundamental change in the strategy.
It abolishes discounts and does not want to chute one collection after another.
The fact that the fashion industry is one of the largest polluters of the environment is nothing new.
Billions of pieces of clothing end up in incinerators and landfills every year.
However, the view of slow fashion, an often despised approach to dressing, has changed quite quickly over the last few years.
Approximately half of Pietro Filipi's merchandised goods at full price.
The other half, which was sold due to discounts, they simply do not buy.
The company does not want to churn out one collection after another and at the end of each season to discount products below their production price.
In addition, according to owner Michal Mička, the times of new and old collections and a blind following the fast-changing fashion trends are gone.
"Mayor of retailers already expect discounts when buying goods, so much higher margins are included in the full price.
In fact, it’s kind of a scounduff,” says Pietro Filipi owner Michal Mička.
In addition, with its approach, the company also wants to support the local business.
"Each our product is made from carefully selected quality fabrics and designed by local designers.
Emphasis is placed that a large part of our collection consists of so-called basic pieces - i.e. clothes that are time-mortial and above-season.
Worldwide, the segment of premium brands is the absence of any discounts typical, the price of the product simply corresponds to its quality and the discount is not needed," Mička adds.
Giant graffiti introduces RAF aviator Kurta Taussig
"The task was to create something for the anniversary of the end of the war.
And I was reminded of our fighter-powered pilot, who was bakering in the ranks of the British Royal Air Force in World War II.
The proposal was enjoyed by the city leadership," said Vacek, who completed his work after a fortnight of intensive work.
It consumed about 300 liters of paint and 50 sprays.
Kurt Taussig was born in 1923 in Teplice-Šanov.
He left the city when he was 15 years old.
During the war, due to his Jewish diffraubilities draged him to an internment camp, where he was held for several weeks.
However, he and his brother managed to escalate a concentration camp thanks to Nicholas Winton's organization.
Taussig made it to the U.N., where he voluntarily joined the Royal Air Force (RAF), and then underwent pilot training in South Rhodesia and Palestine.
He ensengaged in combat operations at the beginning of 1945.
After the Velvet Revolution several times he also visited his native Teplice, which gave him honorary citizenship.
Thirty-six-year-old Kamil Vacek, who has been known in the field of street graphic art under the nickname Camo, has been working with sprinkles in Teplice streets for more than 20 years.
It started with graffiti on surfaces set aside for these purposes by the then mayor Jaroslav Kubera (ODS).
Threte Hanna arrives at the coast of Texas
Hurricane Hanna on the Saturday afternoon local time (before midnight CELCH last night) arrived on the south-west coast of the U.S. state of Texas.
He hit the texas island of Padre Island, about 24 kilometers north of Port Mansfield and weakened into a tropic thunderstorm during its movement over the mainland.
According to meteorologists, it still poses a great danger to the inhabitants of coastal areas.
Strong winds and intensive rainfall have brought with them; along with local flooding, power outbursts and various material damage have been reported, including damaged ships.
Texas Gov. Greg Abbott declared a state of natural catastrophe on Saturday for 32 county districts in south-South Texas and has recommended voluntary evacuation.
The strongest warnings were given to people from the coastal belt between Port Mansfield and Mesquite Bay Bay, but in a total of almost 500 kilometers the environment, before gradually moving over northeastern Mexico.
"While Hanna is now a tropic storm, heavy rain and wind, rising levels, dangerous surf and isolated tornadoes can pose a threat," the National Hurricane Center (NHC) said.
Earlier this morning local time (afternoon CEST), the ruptuor was moving in an U.S.-Muxican border area and was accompanied by winds of 85 kilometers per hour.
Hanna became the first hurricane of this year's hurricane season in the Atlantic region on Saturday.
Above the Gulf of Mexico, she was accompanied by a wind of up to 145 kilometers per hour, which gradually faded up to 112 kilometers per hour during advancing to the mainland, which again eliminated Hanna from the hurricane category.
Authorities have urged local residents to survive the storm at home without forgetting sanitary measures associated with preventing coronavirus spread.
Texas is one of those U.S. states that plague the covid-19 pandemic most.
Due to the existing security measures and the need to comply with distances, possible evacuations are also a big challenge.
Temporary shelving plants were opened in hotels, schools and gyms.
In the district of Hidalgo of Texas, one of the buildings was also adapted as evacuated shelter who tested positive for the coronavirus test or were otherwise exposed.
Local authorities have urged all evacuees to take masks and disinfectanciate gels with them if possible.
"We don't even have it over yet.
Expect catastrophic mirrheatures," Chris Birchfield, who works for the National Meteorological Service (NWWS) service in Brownsville.
Predictions argue that in some places it could rain up to 300 millimetres of precipitation in a few hours, in some cases almost 500 millimetres, which, together with sanctuations of sea level, could flood a number of settlements on the coast.
Zeman lends general rank to Colonel Lipko
President Miloš Zeman on Wednesday will lend the general rank to Colonel Pavel Lipka.
That's according to an outcreation of the expected events of the Ministry of Defence.
The denomination of the rank of brigadiate general is the August accession of Lipka to the post of the deputy commander of the allied multinational division northeast in Poland.
Lipka still acts as the deputy commander of the ground forces of the Czech army.
Zeman was promoted to Zeman at the end of June by the government.
Since the beginning of August, Lipka will replace Brigaddied General Karel Řehka as the deputy commander of the multinational division northeast in Elblag, Poland, which commands the units of an allied pre-shielded presence on the territory of Poland and the Baltics.
Řehka in March became the director of the National Office for Cyber and Information Security (NÚKIB).
The government has proposed to the president to lion Lipko the rank of brigadier general.
Pursuant to the Act on Professional Soldiers, a higher rank can be lent to a Soldier for the duration of the elected office or for the period of performance of an official task, which requires the lending of a higher rank.
The position of the deputy commander of the alliance division corresponds to the general's rank.
Soldier to whom the rank has been lent has the same rights and culpations as if he had been appointed to that rank.
However, he is not entitled to an adjustment of salaries corresponding to a given rank.
Primation of the rank takes the waiver to overlook the reason for which it was provided.
"Lucluker Lipka demonstrates high professional readiness and is a model for subordinates.
Significantly participates in fulfilling the tasks of the Armed Forces of the Czech Republic, including collaboration with foreign partners and ensuring the participation of the army in foreign operations," the Ministry of Defense said in June on a proposal for promotion.
Wednesday's promotion will take place at 12:30 a.m. at the Prague Castle, it will be attended by Defense Minister Lubomír Metnar and Chief of the General Staff Aleš Opata.
EU countries have coronavirus aviation standards
The countries of the European Union have agreed on common hygiene standards for air travel to help curb the propagation of the new coronavirus.
This was stated by German Transport Minister Andreas Scheuer.
Among the stipulated standards there is the observance of the distance or wearing of drapes on the plane and at the aerodrome, but the middle seats on the planes will not have to remain vacancied.
This was reported by the Reuters news agency.
"I'm delighted that my colleagues have adopted the German proposal for Europe and that we were able to agree on these unequivocal standards," Scheuer said.
The German Ministry of Transport then added that the agreement was concluded by officials and that it has yet to be formally approved by ministers.
Agreed measures include the requirement to cover your mouth and nose for passengers from the age of six, observe the recommended distance during security check-ups and check-in at the airport.
There must also be plenty of fresh air on airplanes, and all information must be available in several languages.
The airlines will also be able to occupy the middle seats.
The common provisions agreed upon by officials meet at least some of the requirements of the airlines.
Distinguished standards caused confusion.
On Arctic Svalbard, a temperature record was scored, measuring almost 22 degrees
"A 41-year-a-old record has been set in Longyearbyen," the Norwegian Meteorological Institute said on Twitter.
In the early evening the temperature rose to 21.7 degrees Celsius, surpassing by 0.4-degree records from 1979, he added.
Roughly two-thousuous Longyearbyen is the largest city on Svalbard, located about 1300 kilometers from the North Pole.
Svalbard raked faster than almost any other place on Earth.
This excerpts the dangers arising from the warming of other regions of the Arctic - from Alaska to Siberia, according to a report by the Norwegian Centre for Climate Studies last year.
According to him, the average temperature on Svalbard has risen by three to five degrees since the 70's of the 20th century.
By 2100, it could even rise by ten degrees, as long as greenhouse gas is not lowered into the atmosphere.
Temperature growth can result in melting eternally frozen soil in SVicberks, endangering the stability of many buildings, roads or airports and can cause even greater avaluations and landslides.
Climatic warming also threatens Arctic fauna, such as polar bears or seals, which are dependent on the ocean's icidrification.
Britons returning from Spain awaits mandatory two-week quarantine
