The Czech Foreign Ministry has newly highlighted an increased risk of contagion in Catalonia, Aragon, Baskick and the area of Madrid.
The model of the purchase of electricity from Dukovany will be negotiated by the government
On Monday, the government will discuss the model of the purchase of electricity from the planned new unit of the Dukovany nuclear power plant.
The bill envisages that in the event that the feed-of-commit price is higher than the price of power electricity in the market, the difference will be paid by all consumers through tariffs.
In the latter case, when the feed-of-commit price will be lower than the price on the market, the impact will be the opposite.
Strong electricity is an unregulated part from the final price of electricity for customers.
Vice-Prime Minister Karel Havlíček (for ANO) and the CEZ energy company have previously stated that a more likely option is that people will save money thanks to electricity construction.
Analytists, on the contrary, point out that the long-term outlook for electricity prices in the markets is always uncertain, which developments have shown several times in the past.
Eco-measurers have previously criticized the intent.
"The feed-in price can be both below the market price of power electricity and above it.
The final impact at the time of applicability of the rule can thus be both negative and positive.
The impact degrades between customers according to the size of the circuit breaker - at lower consumption or according to the consumption of electricity - at higher electricity consumption," the Ministry of Industry and Trade (MPO) said in the materials.
Havlíček stated in May that he expects that the wholesale price of electricity will rise and people will make a profit on Dukovany.
In the opposite case, the impact in the tens of crowns per year for the family, according to him, is much less than people pay annually in invoices for electricity to support so-called supported sources.
Earlier, he estimated that the feed-in price could range from 50 to 60 euros (about 1320 to 1600 CZK) per mega-watt-hour (MWh).
Submitted material does not contain any proposal of the amounts, it should only be included in the buy-out contract.
The price of electricity on the Prague Energy Stock Exchange for the year 2021, to which traders are now most stocked, is currently around 45 euros (approximately CZK 1190) per MWh.
"With the difference in the feed-in price and the market price of power electricity of ten euros, the surcharge of the average household with a consumption of 3.3 MWh per year could correspond to a four-member household, standardly equipped with electrical appliances including an electric hob and an oven, which, however, does not use electricity for heating and hot water - less than 100 CZK/year," she wrote in the documents for the government.
"We are living in an extremely turbulent time.
The coronavirus pandemic has shown us how vulnerabilable the global economy is.
For this reason, I consider the long-term prediction of market prices to be explicitly speculative matters," Tomáš Voltr, executive director of the energy financial group, told the Czech News Agency.
The proposed law envisages that the purchase would be carried out by an MIT, or a legal entity authorized by the state at a predetermined price.
The contract between the investor and the state, guaranteeing the conditions for the purchase of electricity, would be concluded for at a minimum of 30 years, with a possible extension by ten years, even repetition, but no later than until the closure of low-carbon production.
The low-canded design is defined as an electricity generating plant with a nuclear reactor connected to the electricity system with an installed capacity with a minimum 100 mega-watt (MW) after 2030.
Havlíček previously stated that the new Dukovany block should have an output of 1200 MW and produce nine terawatt-hours of electricity a year, which would correspond to the tenth expected Czech consumption.
The bloc should be put into operation in 2036.
The model of financing of the new Dukovany bloc was approved by the government on Monday.
Establishment should be financed by an interest-employment accident during construction, two percent interest is expected during operation.
Opposition does not disagree with the financing model, it is also bothered by the possibility that Dukovany would be built by Russian or Chinese firms.
The state should contribute 70 per cent in the construction.
The rest should be paid by CEZ, which will also cover all possible additional costs.
CEZ CEO Daniel Beneš told ČTK in May that the expected sum for the construction of the block at the current prices is about six billion euros (approximately 162 billion crowns).
Contracts between the state and CEZ on the construction of a new Dukovany bloc will be signed on Tuesday.
The Ministry of Regional Development has withdraw material about the support of tourism in the media for two billion
Submitted two billion kroners by the Ministry of Regional Development (MMMR), according to which the Ministry of Regional Development (MMR) planned to spend two billion crowns in the media, on the Internet and billboards for advertising on vacation in the Czech Republic.
Minister Klára Dostálová (for ANO) told SeznamZprávy.cz that the material is still being worked on.
Zdopravy.cz drew attention to the document in eKlep (electronic Library of the Legislative Process) on Friday.
The ministry justified the plan with a slump of tourism due to the coronavirus.
During the coronavirus crisis there was a slump in revenue and media.
The proposal provoked criticism of the opposition and representatives of tourism.
According to the proposal, which can now be consulted on the website hlidacstatu.cz, the MMR has planned to give money for advertising in television, radio, the press, on the Internet, billboards or surfaces at railway stations.
The largest share of the money, 700 million kroner, would have been received by television.
The majority would have been acquired by private broadcasters Nova and Prima.
Printed dailies and weeklies would get $400 million, and MMR would give the MMR for PR articles, advertising banners and videos on the website.
Radio stations would get $350 million for the media campaign, most of it again private stations.
The remaining 150 million would be used for outdoor advertisements on billboards, LED screens and surfaces at railway stations or in the subway.
Campaign to support tourism in the Czech Republic was supposed to start in mid-August and last a year.
"The material is still being worked on, it's definitely not in the form that should be presented yet," Dostálová said without any further details of the Seznam News server.
The material was criticized by the opposition, according to the head of TOP 09 Markéta Pekarová Adamová by the media from the trust funds of Prime Minister Andrej Babiš (ANO).
"I firmly believe that this ostentatious contemplation will get a blow between his eyes in the autumn of the elections," she tweeted.
Dostálová denys that in fact it is about helping the media affected by the crisis.
"It could also be a coincidence, I don't even know anything about media support," she told Deník N on Friday.
According to her, the two-and-dollar budget is indicative.
"Of course we're still counting it, it could be less or more.
It's working material," she added.
The Saviour of Tourism initiative does not make sense to support domestic tourism.
"We want two-billion for companies in tourism, not an unnecessary advertising campaign," Tomáš Barčík, chairman of the initiative, said on Sunday.
If there are those two billion crowns in the state budget, they should be directed to companies, not to the media, the initiative writes in an open letter to the government.
