Duplantis has a millennium lead, it's a bump in the ass, laments Swede challenger
The 22-year-old American sticker has ambitions and the potential to be one of the most esteemed competitors of the new ruler.
He already knows what it's like to beat Duplantis two-year-legged, last year he was defeated at a meeting of NCAA Universities.
"We've had a battle a year before, that's when he won and I was the second, then we switched.
In both cases it was a very good duel," describes Nilsen, who secured the victory with a 5.95 metre.
But Duplantis has been fissing steeply up since then, and at the moment he finds himself alone.
At the age of just twenty, he became a world record-breaker, surpassing the 6.17-metre lane in Torun on Feb. on February 8 in Torun; a week later he added a centimeter.
"They were jumping six meters already at the age of 18 when he graduated from high school," Nilsen recalled for World Athletics.
"It was clear that as soon as the boy came into a man, he would multiply all his potential and talent tenfold.
Twenty-five years is a pretty good old age to jump a world record," he said.
Although Nilsen's list of achievements is noticeably dwindler than the one Duplantis prides itself, an American sets similar goals.
So far he has hinted at the NCAA races, which he regularly won, in February this year he improved the internal personal record to 5.93 meters. Last August he won a gold medal at the Pan-American Games in Lima, already in 2017 he made it to the U.S. expedition to the world championship, where he finished 13.
The closest milestone for him for the first time is to taste the Diamant League.
