A woman has spent hundreds of hundreds of thousands from a disabled son's disability pension.
It awaits a trial
The money the 33-year-old man receives from the state as an invalidity pension is intended primarily to improve the quality of his life.
For embezzlement, the woman faces up to eight years in prison.
Due to ill-health of health, the court stripped the sick man of legal capacity and appointed his mother a culinary officer, who, among other things, had to manage his son's money.
"The woman from mid-November 2013 to January this year, a woman used a part of the funds from the invalidity pension of the son for her own use and deprived him of 440 thousand crowns," police spokeswoman Dana Ladmanová said, accustoming the defendant to aggravating the accused woman that as a custodian she had a particularly obligation to defend the interests of the injured party.
According to the order of the court, the woman set up an account for her son, but his pension was sent to her account.
"After paying the costs associated with the stay of her son in a medical facility and the reimbursement of necessary drugs, she used the remaining funds for the purchase of a vehicle, electrical appliances and for the operation of the household," the indictment said.
That she couldn't use her son's money for her own needs, the woman admitted.
However, she argued by the fact that she assisted them in the payment of family expenses.
“We have had trouble tightening the running of the household.
We've never bought anything above-standard.
It's always been for things whose purchase the son would have approved.
I considered it a family money.
I didn't have any idea I was committing a felony," the woman explained to the police.
Promised that she would return the money to her son.
North America has inhabited for 30 000 years, scientists say after the discovery of tools
Findings from Chiquihuite Cave in the Mexican state of Zacatecas include 1930 lime liquor tools, among which there are delicate blades that could have been used to slic meat, and pointed objects that could serve as spikes of spears or spears.
The researchers found that the oldest of the tools came from 33 000 to 31,000 years ago.
"There are only a few products of this old age.
But they're here," said archaeologist Ciprian Ardelean of the University of Zacatecas, and the lead author of one of the two studies that were published in the journal Nature.
The youngest instruments reach the age of 12,500 years of age.
According to the experts, people inhabited Chiquihuite Cave, located at a high altitude of 20 000 years.
So far the generally recognized theory has talked about the fact that the first representatives of Homo sapiens had crossed the mainland bridge from today's Russia to Alaska about 13,500 years ago.
Archaeological evidence, including unique spear spikes used to kill mammoths and other prehistoric animals, have shown that this mayor culture subsequently spread in much of North America.
However, this model has received rifts in recent decades when several ancient settlements have been uncovered, which were two to three thousand years older.
In the second study published in the journal Nature, there are 42 different localities throughout North America, which prove human presence on the continent as early as 26,000 to 19,000 years ago.
Scientists believe that a small number of people may have come to the North American mainland earlier than had been anticipated so far.
Some of them could get to America by boat.
"It is clear that people were in America long before the development of the so-called mayor culture," anthropologist Ruth Gruhn, an anthropologist at the University of Alberta, said in a statement.
Interest in airfamiliar flights so far fulfils an optimistic scenario, according to sellers.
