Coronavirus cases have increased at least since the beginning of the week, the number of tests has decreased
Since the beginning of March, when the new coronavirus was first discovered in the Czech Republic, 15 212 people have been infected with it.
Nearly 9,600 have been cured, 368 patients have died.
There are currently 5 254 people sick.
This is according to data on the health ministry's website.
The daily number of newly confirmed cases this week has surpassed two-hundred-thres of four times in a row.
There were 281 cases on Friday.
On Thursday there were 235 tested, a day earlier 246 and on Tuesday 206.
There were 1,169 infections on the first working day this week.
From covid-19, 9 590 people in the Czech Republic have already cured, i.e. 63 per cent of all infected so far.
For the majority, the disease had a slight course.
There are 151 coronavirus-coronavirus people in the hospital, according to the latest data from the Department of Health.
In the Czech Republic there are several outbreaks of the covid-19 disease.
Karviná still shows the most infected in the last seven days per 100,000 inhabitants, almost 59.
It is followed by the Jihlava district with 56 and Frýdek-Místek with 36 infections per 100,000 inhabitants in the last week.
Due to the increased number of infections, new measures against the spread of the coronavirus have been in place in the Czech Republic since Saturday.
People have to wear drapes at indoor mass events over 100 people, they are cultural and sporting events, as well as weddings and burials inside buildings.
As of Monday, the capacity of these actions will be limited from the current thousands of people to halved.
In the world, the number of people demonstrably infected with the new coronavirus has already exceeded the 16-million mark.
This was stated by Johns Hopkins University, which follows the development of the pandemic globally.
The death tolls who have died with the covid-19 disease are approaching 645 thousand, according to the source.
The most recent million infected were added to statistics in just three days; the 15-million mark fell on July 22.
The U.S., which already registers more than 4.1 million infected, has had the largest share of the rapid growth in the number of cases in recent days, Brazil of 2.4 million and India, where nearly 1.4 million people became infected, according to the authorities.
Infections are now increasing at a record rate.
The World Health Organization announced this week that more than 284,000 new cases worldwide from Thursday to Friday, the highest daily balance since the pandemic began.
This development can be attributed to the fact that the spread of the outbreak is at its peak in populous states, such as the U.S. (Branchair and India), according to Reuters, the number of new cases has risen in nearly 40 countries around the world last week.
This applies, for example, to Australia, Japan, Bolivia, Sudan, Bulgaria, Belgium and Israel.
Contagion has already emerged in almost all countries of the world, the first case was officially announced by the DPRK today.
The air in the home is up to five times inferior to the outside.
Harms air fresheners and deodorants
Health threatens not only poor air quality in outdoor areas, but also at home.
This was pointed out by the Israeli human health expert Carol Abraham quoted by The Jerusalem Post.
According to Abraham, who contributed to the creation of the Israeli Ministry of Environmental Conservation, it is necessary to focus on harmful substances in households right now, when a lot of people spend more time working from home.
Abraham referred to the research of the U.S. Environment Agencies (EPA), according to which the quality of domestic air is up to five times inferior to the outdoor.
The World Health Organization (WHO) believes 3.8 million people die prematurely every year due to poor air quality in their homes.
"Due to the coronavirus outbreak, which has forburssed a lot and is still for forcing work from home, people have to spend more hours in their homes than before, so it's time to focus on indoor air quality," she said.
According to her, almost no one deals with the quality of "home air" in Israel.
Dangerous can be things that a person routinely comes into contact - paints, air fresheners, disinfection agents, deodorants or pesticides.
"They also disembark the poisonous gas with garments, which we regularly bring to the dry cleaners," Abrahamova said.
Most endangered, according to her, children, elderly household members and people with breathing difficulties.
Otherwise, healthy individuals can begin to suffer from problems such as headaches, swoes, irritancy of the nasal passages, bleeding from the nose, vomiting or skin allergies.
With prolonged exposure to unhealthy substances, the internal organs and the central nervous system can be damaged.
However, not all harmful substances come from objects within households, the source can also be the material from which the houses are built.
